WEBVTT

00:05.522 --> 00:28.130
[Kaspar König]: You are listening to Clap to COLAB on Collaboradio.org as a part of Freie Radios Berlin Brandenburg streaming on fr-bb.org and Collaboradio.org transmitting on DHB plus channel 7D in Berlin and channel 12D in Brandenburg.

00:29.651 --> 00:46.699
[Kaspar König]: In this radio show, sound artist Kaspar König presents interviews and sound recordings from Colab, Technobiological Futures, a project by the artist-run Bio and Hacking Labs, hosted on Hackteria.org and sponsored by Pro Helvetia.

00:47.900 --> 00:59.648
[Kaspar König]: Listeners are invited into a global network of makers, exploring the collective motivations behind the work and the unique context of this collaborative lab culture.

00:59.648 --> 01:09.195
[Kaspar König]: The show is edited and produced between Zurich and the Emmental, so expect a few holes here and there, both in the sound and the narrative.

01:10.556 --> 01:19.103
[Kaspar König]: Alongside uncovering these collaborations, the episode dips into music and sound art, made in the DIWO spirit.

01:19.103 --> 01:20.964
[Kaspar König]: Do it with others.

01:20.964 --> 01:29.511
[Kaspar König]: Kaspar König himself, a sound artist, also shares his fascination with unexpected sounds and surprising discoveries from the workshop.

01:29.511 --> 01:29.932
[Kaspar König]: Enjoy!

02:22.515 --> 02:22.639
Bye.

03:05.533 --> 03:05.697
Yeah.

04:50.966 --> 04:55.330
[Kaspar König]: Dear listeners, I'm going to introduce you to Marc Dusseiller.

04:55.330 --> 05:02.856
[Kaspar König]: Marc is, I would say the initiator of CoLabs and I would love to ask him some questions.

05:02.856 --> 05:04.317
[Kaspar König]: Mark, are you ready for this?

05:04.317 --> 05:06.439
[Kaspar König]: Yes, I am.

05:06.439 --> 05:07.640
[Kaspar König]: Always been.

05:07.640 --> 05:09.382
[Kaspar König]: Yeah, tell me a little bit about you.

05:09.382 --> 05:11.884
[Kaspar König]: Can you tell your own name maybe and introduce yourself?

05:12.343 --> 05:39.420
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: yeah um yeah my name is Marc you introduce me with my full name Marc Dusseiller but i have many names they also call me dusjagr on the internet um other people call me Pakde Marcjono or now since i was in thailand they also call me Lung Ma which means uncle mark i just turned 50 recently my hair turned gray so the way i'm perceived now on my international travels is more like the uncle and not kind of as a

05:40.255 --> 05:42.896
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: other young boy and crazy traveler anymore.

05:42.896 --> 05:47.038
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: But yeah, I'm also enjoying this role.

05:47.038 --> 06:06.185
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: I have a background in science, but I've been kind of really following this interface of art, technology, science and society on a very global scale the last, I would say almost 20 years already, that I've been interacting with people in various parts of the world, bringing people together from different backgrounds.

06:08.565 --> 06:14.768
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: for example, art and science, but also different backgrounds from different cultural environments.

06:14.768 --> 06:17.670
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: I called myself a workshopologist.

06:17.670 --> 06:22.853
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: We came up with this word long time ago, also in the context of Hackteria.

06:22.853 --> 06:34.019
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: We turned everything into a workshop, and we then also termed this word workshopology, like reflecting on how we share knowledge through kind of workshops.

06:34.019 --> 06:37.000
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: And then I started to call myself a workshopologist.

06:39.537 --> 06:58.682
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: Besides organizing events and organizing spaces here in Zurich, I think my main means of cultural output is workshops, either as organizing workshops for others with others or also doing the workshops myself.

06:58.682 --> 06:59.562
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: Yeah, that's who I am.

07:00.503 --> 07:02.144
[Kaspar König]: Well, thank you so much.

07:02.144 --> 07:17.054
[Kaspar König]: Because I believe you have an incredible interest in bringing these people together on a purpose-based idea, but basically it's deeper.

07:17.054 --> 07:22.357
[Kaspar König]: It's about you want to find that deeper connection over time, or am I wrong?

07:23.505 --> 07:27.009
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: Good point.

07:27.009 --> 07:39.043
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: On one hand I'm kind of a bit part of the internet, the early internet generation kind of using digital tools to share knowledge, like call it kind of stuff like Wikipedia has influenced me in my young years.

07:40.112 --> 07:50.314
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: that we can share digital knowledge, but then the last years I'm really into a kind of a human in-person interaction much more and also more durational.

07:50.314 --> 07:57.036
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: A lot of my collaborations and partnerships also turned into friendships long-term.

07:57.036 --> 08:06.938
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: So I've been working with the same groups and the same individuals, many of them for long-term and also these workshop situations or gathering situations

08:08.172 --> 08:18.377
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: I really like it when they're like durational long, like so you can really make human connections over several weeks maybe and pick them up again years later.

08:18.377 --> 08:26.501
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: You are an example of the first workshop we ever did with Hackteria was in Berlin and you were my collaborator.

08:26.501 --> 08:29.743
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: I think it was just when we first got to know each other here in Zurich.

08:30.442 --> 08:33.284
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: And it's nice that we still collaborate.

08:33.284 --> 08:36.587
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: And that was a workshop on kind of building microscopes.

08:36.587 --> 08:43.873
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: And you joined to also sonify those microscopic environments with microphones.

08:43.873 --> 08:46.515
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: And it was also a one week workshop.

08:46.515 --> 08:48.616
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: It was not like a one day kind of fast food.

08:48.616 --> 08:54.081
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: It was also experimenting together, having an interaction with other people from

08:55.123 --> 08:57.630
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: from the city yeah and a bit of a joke also

09:04.945 --> 09:08.686
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: that we picked up a few years ago is like make friends, not art.

09:08.686 --> 09:13.587
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: So organizing, we call it co-lapse, but the full name is co-laboratories.

09:13.587 --> 09:31.392
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: You can also call it group residences, bringing people together for several weeks to also, as I said, beyond just like developing a new project or finishing an art piece, it's really also making friends that hopefully then collaborate years later again.

09:58.507 --> 09:58.527
[Music Playing]: ... ...

10:46.085 --> 10:46.146
[Music Playing]: you

