[Music Playing]: of the

11:55.205 --> 11:55.486
Let's go.

12:41.672 --> 12:42.095
Oh

13:19.212 --> 13:41.045
[Kaspar König]: That might explain also the title of the project, Technobiological Futures.

13:42.028 --> 13:44.251
[Kaspar König]: the collaboration in that sense.

13:44.251 --> 13:48.617
[Kaspar König]: Will you relate that to this?

13:48.617 --> 13:55.187
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: Hackteria and also personally I've been always interested in this interface of technology and the living.

13:57.129 --> 14:11.617
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: bridging brains to computers with some implanted microchips or my science fiction kind of visions and also building microscopes to look at worms or connecting human beings to technologies.

14:11.617 --> 14:20.142
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: So it's also something I think the last whatever 10 years it's merging more and more

14:20.718 --> 14:47.891
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: I think technology becomes really part of the body about human life, we're merging, you know, the whole planet is like kind of technologically influenced by human innovations and we want to talk about this interface of technology and living and we also want to speculate together in these interdisciplinary groups what kind of future we want in this merging of technology and the living

14:49.482 --> 14:51.963
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: So that was kind of the title.

14:51.963 --> 14:55.304
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: So we're not interested in purely screen-based digital art.

14:55.304 --> 15:01.227
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: We're also not like just farmers that kind of make some nice gardens in the neighborhood.

15:01.227 --> 15:14.392
[Marc Dusseiller aka dusjagr]: So we want to kind of critically and creatively look at this interface of technology and biology from the laboratory, but also from the farm and also play with technologies and computers at the same time.

15:52.935 --> 15:54.836
[Kaspar König]: Clap to COLAB.

15:54.836 --> 16:06.919
[Kaspar König]: It's a radio show that documents the beautiful network Collab, Technobiological Futures project initiated by Mark Dusey, that we just heard in the earlier part of the show.

16:06.919 --> 16:13.902
[Kaspar König]: And actually we're going to continue to listen to an interview with Adam Zuretsky, part of their network as well.

16:13.902 --> 16:19.403
[Kaspar König]: And he is telling you about the Beak Lab.

16:19.403 --> 16:20.624
[Adam Zaretsky]: I am Adam Zuretsky.

16:21.856 --> 16:25.097
[Adam Zaretsky]: The core of my lab is wet and juicy.

16:25.097 --> 16:51.128
[Adam Zaretsky]: I prefer to try to bring the bioart into the biotechnological futures by doing workshops, hands-on workshops, where people feel and experience the danger and beauty of new biotechnologies, especially leading into genetic modification of the human and the animal and the non-human.

16:52.698 --> 16:58.282
[Adam Zaretsky]: But also not just genetically modifying, but thinking, why are we doing this?

16:58.282 --> 17:08.831
[Adam Zaretsky]: What is the goal and how close to extinction velocity are we getting through engineering of life?

17:08.831 --> 17:17.038
[Adam Zaretsky]: According to some perceived need or stranger yet, just stylistic intention.

18:59.068 --> 19:02.730
[Adam Zaretsky]: Oh, good question.

19:02.730 --> 19:15.398
[Adam Zaretsky]: Best growing things in my lab are embryos, human embryos, DNA cupcakes, strange new human bodies.

19:15.398 --> 19:27.965
[Adam Zaretsky]: We're working best at tissue culture or organ culture or incubation and alteration of embryonic

19:29.070 --> 19:40.920
[Adam Zaretsky]: Goo, germ cells, sperm, eggs, all of these grow really well, even full term babies sometimes mutant babies grow really well in my lab.

