34:36.542 --> 34:47.589
[Adam Zaretsky]: Oh, the most important thing is that we talk about the aesthetics and the ethics of redesigning organisms, in particular, human gene editing.

34:47.589 --> 34:49.911
[Adam Zaretsky]: And when we talk about the ethics,

34:50.907 --> 35:01.151
[Adam Zaretsky]: In the terms of aesthetics, we run automatically into the horrors of eugenics, which is trying to decide who is best and who should die.

35:01.151 --> 35:11.135
[Adam Zaretsky]: And right now we have all these transhumanist oligarchs that want to make the perfect babies and make these perfect babies outlive all the other babies and replace them with the

35:12.305 --> 35:14.526
[Adam Zaretsky]: usually the sperm of the oligarchs.

35:14.526 --> 35:16.426
[Adam Zaretsky]: But this is a founder effect.

35:16.426 --> 35:19.387
[Adam Zaretsky]: This is kind of like a heritage act.

35:19.387 --> 35:24.928
[Adam Zaretsky]: But I'm not into this sort of neo-fascist breeding.

35:24.928 --> 35:41.553
[Adam Zaretsky]: And so I'm interested in the aesthetics of otherness, the aesthetics of non-perfection, and in particular, the aesthetics of your even ugly punk babies.

